# Line endings are otherwise deliberately unmanaged. The index does not depend
# on them: the preprocessor normalizes CRLF to LF before segmenting, so object
# keys derive from normalized content (spec §6.1). Verified, not assumed — the
# pinned ripgrep corpus is checked out CRLF on Windows and LF elsewhere, and
# both produce the same object keys. rustfmt accepts either, and Rust's
# `str::lines()` strips a trailing CR, so the eval JSONL parses either way.
#
# A shell script is the one file whose bytes a program other than ours reads:
# CRLF makes the shebang `#!/usr/bin/env bash^M`, which fails as a bad
# interpreter.
*.sh text eol=lf
